# Springboard example: an agent that must know when to stop.
#
# This is the shape of agent you'd build with Leashterm instead of a
# wall-clock timeout, a request-count quota, or a human watching the job and
# killing it: the bound is not infrastructure sitting *around* the agent's
# code, it is a property of the code itself. There is no `while` in
# Leashterm at all, so an agent CANNOT write an unbounded "keep checking
# until ready" loop even if it wanted to - the only repetition available is
# `retry N` (a fixed N from 1 to 10) and `for` over a list written out in
# the program. On top of that, the v0.7 step budget (`--max-steps`, default
# 10,000) bounds the total work of the whole program, so even a very long,
# fully spelled-out `for` loop over hundreds of items still has a hard
# ceiling the operator sets, not the agent.
#
# This matters because of a specific, real failure mode: the July 2026
# OpenAI post-mortem on the Hugging Face incident names "excessive
# persistence" as part of what let the incident escalate - agents that kept
# working at a task long after a well-bounded agent would have stopped and
# reported failure. A language that cannot express "keep trying forever" is
# a structural answer to that, not a policy one.
#
# Extend this: add more `retry`/`for` blocks for your own bounded work, and
# replace the fixed check below with whatever the agent decides to do (an
# LLM call, in a real system, would generate that logic) - it can never stop
# being bounded no matter what it generates.

needs read("data/check_1.txt")

let status = trim(read("data/check_1.txt"))
if status == "pending" {
  print("not ready yet, giving up after one check (this language has no way to check forever)")
} else {
  print(status)
}
